      "side": 1,
      "message": "Per TCPCI spec `4.4.2 ALERT Register (Normative)`, this will not de-assert the RX_BUF_OVF alert. Only RX_STATUS will if both are cleared in one I2C write.\n\nHowever, TCPCI does not explicitly state RX_STATUS is set on overflow. An excessive unchunked message may not fit in the RX buffer, but would cause an overflow.\n\nExample:\n\n* TCPC FIFO 128 bytes. RX_BUF populated with 126 byte message. RX_STATUS set.\n* Assume ALERT# interrupt handler hasn\u0027t fired yet.\n* New 4 byte message arrives. RX overflows. RX_BUF_OVF is set.\n* Assume ALERT# handler now fires and begins running.\n\nOr:\n\n* TCPC FIFO 128 bytes. RX_BUF unpopulated/empty. RX_STATUS unset.\n* New 160 byte message arrives. RX overflows. RX_BUF_OVF is set.\n* Assume ALERT# handler fires. (Now RX_STATUS bit is undefined/anomalous.)\n\nLogic must be robust enough to acknowledge the overflow, without flushing the RX buffer. Merely adding RX_BUF_OVF as conditional to L1226 while() loop may not achieve this.",
